A component abstraction should stay **ordinary Moth data**. Its author chooses
the public type and const functions. A page decides where that data becomes
output.

### A deliberately small result type

This is an illustrative source-backed `@html` design, not a committed
constructor or public signature:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    HtmlPart = |
        body String = "",
        stylesheets String = "",
    |

    html_page #|parts {HtmlPart}| -> HtmlPart:
        body ~= ""
        stylesheets ~= ""

        loop parts |part|:
            ~body = [body, part.body]
            ~stylesheets = [stylesheets, part.stylesheets]
        ;

        return HtmlPart(
            body = body,
            stylesheets = stylesheets
        )
    ;
]

A template is the normal way to concatenate Strings in Moth. The composite
result carries HTML and a structural String of stylesheet references. It holds
no hidden page registration, renderer instance or component lifecycle.

A real library will also need a way to carry independent `head` contributions.
That representation remains an API decision. Concatenating head snippets into
one String loses the boundaries the builder needs to deduplicate them
individually, so treat `HtmlPart` as a starting shape rather than a finished
contract.

### Keep local slots local

The Basic example's `card` owns its named `title` slot. An imported or reused
card cannot reach an enclosing page's builder slots. That parent-only routing
rule keeps deep composition predictable and stops an intermediate component
from silently changing where inserts go.

Const functions compose finished `HtmlPart` values across file and module
boundaries. They run under the compiler's constant rules, never call runtime DOM
functions and cannot invent new declarations or source code.

### Carry resources, not generated URLs

A component's `stylesheets` field can come from file values such as
`@sidebar.css`. Composition preserves the resource origin inside the String.
The builder later resolves URLs for the destination document and deduplicates
stylesheets by stable origin, keeping first-use order.

One builder contract still needs implementation. Today's `stylesheets` slot
accepts exactly **one** CSS resource per direct insert. The composed
multi-resource String on the Basic page describes an intended extension under
investigation, not what the current slot validator accepts. That validator must
keep each CSS origin and refuse stray text, site-root pieces and non-CSS
resources.

Don't compare resource strings to test whether two components use the same
file. A URL depends on the output context. CSS rules shared by every instance
belong in a separate resource. Inline `$css` suits one instance's small value,
such as a CSS custom property.

### Types and boundaries keep reuse safe

A nominal `HtmlPart` gives the library a checked contract. It never converts an
arbitrary record automatically. Const construction checks every field and every
called function. Reusable source packages publish only selected declarations
through `$export`.

Runtime components and browser handles still obey Moth's shared and exclusive
access rules and mandatory lifetime analysis. Returning a String from a const
function grants no retained DOM reference and no implicit subscription.
Explicit `Error!` contracts and ordinary recovery handle expected runtime
failures. A const function that the compiler cannot evaluate reports a
diagnostic instead of quietly becoming runtime work.

### When to use the pattern

Reach for a reusable `HtmlPart` result when a navbar, sidebar, tabbed docs
section or pager owns markup plus resource dependencies. A straightforward
article can stay a direct top-level `["[$md: ...]"]` fragment. Keep runtime
behaviour in a separately invoked function, covered in
@../browser-behaviour/ (Browser behaviour).

See @../../constants (Constants), @../../resources (Resources) and
@../../packages/core/html/ (@html).
